WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.890
- Everybody keeps talking
about QUIET QUITTING.

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.068
- QUIET QUITTING.

00:00:03.068 --> 00:00:05.460
- Have you heard the
phrase, QUIET QUITTING?

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.777
- [Narrator] In 2022,
American workers say,

00:00:07.777 --> 00:00:10.290
"They are taking a step
back from their jobs,

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.477
which isn't great news for the economy.

00:00:12.477 --> 00:00:14.250
- The prosperity of the whole country

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:17.580
and the prosperity for you and me,

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.710
depends on productivity.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.050
- [Narrator] US worker
productivity in 2022,

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:25.350
had the steepest drop
in the last 74 years.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:26.820
Here's why labor productivity

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.830
is so central to the economy.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:29.910
And why big drops,

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:32.057
can be difficult to recover from.

00:00:32.057 --> 00:00:35.107
(upbeat music)

00:00:35.107 --> 00:00:37.050
"Productivity is the amount of output

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.210
of goods and services that workers produce

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:41.700
for every hour they put into their jobs."

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.290
What most economists look at

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:44.580
is the productivity rate,

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:45.510
which is calculated

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:49.290
by the Bureau of Labor Statistics or BLS.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.360
The BLS finds the productivity rate,

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.220
by dividing the national output

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.200
by total labor hours.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.520
It might seem simple,

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.700
but actually, there are
a lot of factors at play.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.710
- So there's a lot of inputs,

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.980
and a lot of data that
get revised later on,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.320
that make it one of the
more complicated statistics

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.730
to put together.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.140
- [Narrator] Going back to the equation,

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.030
let's look at labor hours.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.880
That includes hours worked
by wage and salary workers,

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.270
the self-employed, and
unpaid family workers.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.460
The other input, national output,

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.280
comes from the Bureau
of Economic Analysis.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.627
The main factor is Gross Domestic Product,

00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:27.210
"A measure of the nation's output

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.100
of all goods and services."

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.750
Including things like food,

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.850
gas, and healthcare,

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.880
but the equation also
leaves some things out.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.370
- When officials measure productivity,

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:39.630
they're trying to look at

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.370
the activity of the private sector.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:43.380
So they'll strip out things like farms,

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.930
they'll strip out things like nonprofits.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:46.830
What they're trying to do

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:49.830
is get to the kind of core
of the business sector

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.180
of the United States.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.080
- [Narrator] And it's a metric

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.270
that can be important for workers.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.130
- The productivity of workers

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:59.550
is a key ingredient in creating income,

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:02.370
whether it's corporate income
in the form of profits,

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.117
or whether it's individual income,

00:02:04.117 --> 00:02:06.960
in terms of earnings.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.420
And that really is the path to prosperity.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:10.530
- [Narrator] And because productivity

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.180
is so vital to the economy,

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.360
variations in the rate
can cause repercussions.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.060
Let's look to this drop
here, in the 1970s,

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:19.380
to find out why.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.770
A drop like this can indicate
a PRODUCTIVITY SHOCK.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:24.180
- Well, PRODUCTIVITY SHOCK

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.810
is when something good
happens or bad happens,

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.980
that changes the ability of workers

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:33.090
to produce whatever it is they produced.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:34.440
- [Narrator] There were
a lot of things going on

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:35.730
in the 1970s,

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:39.240
like when OPEC Nations
implemented an embargo in 73',

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.570
pushing upwards prices for oil and gas.

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:44.490
And while those costs were going up,

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:46.320
so was the amount of workers,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.020
as a large influx of
women looked for jobs,

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:51.480
driving a labor surplus.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:52.920
And increased competition

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:55.650
from Japan and Germany disrupted trade.

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.300
All of these factors together,

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.760
led to a productivity slump.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:00.900
But the FEDERAL RESERVE

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.290
responded in a way that
made the situation worse.

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:07.200
- So they pumped a lot of money
into the financial system,

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.360
which lowered interest rates,

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.490
and those lower interest
rates and the more money,

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.800
instead of getting the economy
to grow at a fast rate,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.270
just created inflation.

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:16.260
And underneath it all,

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.570
was that the economy
didn't have the capacity

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.090
to deliver as many goods and services

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:23.040
as it had been doing before.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:23.873
They made a mistake,

00:03:23.873 --> 00:03:26.790
and it made a bad inflation
problem, even worse.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:30.000
- [Narrator] It took the FED
years to fix that problem,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.240
but not all PRODUCTIVITY
SHOCKS are negative.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.820
- In the 1970s, we had a negative shock.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:36.690
In the 1990s,

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:38.520
we had a different kind
of PRODUCTIVITY SHOCK.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.830
- [Narrator] This time,
it was a positive one,

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.860
a tech revolution that led
to rising productivity.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.460
- Indeed, data on our national
economy in recent months,

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.950
are beginning to support the notion

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:51.870
that productivity growth,

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:54.630
the basis for increases in earnings

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.250
is beginning to pick up.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.050
- Computer chips were being produced

00:03:58.050 --> 00:03:59.580
at a faster and faster rate,

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.830
with higher and higher capacity.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.170
The internet became a thing.

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:05.940
Mobile technology advanced.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:09.660
And so worker productivity grew

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:11.790
because of that technological shock,

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.543
and that led to a lot of
benefits for the whole economy.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:16.620
- [Narrator] The high productivity,

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.630
meant businesses could sell more,

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:21.510
pay workers more, and increase profits,

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:24.213
while leaving prices largely unchanged.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:26.670
But it didn't last,

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:29.340
due to a stock market bubble that burst.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:30.540
- It was the bubble.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.525
The bubble has now burst.

00:04:32.525 --> 00:04:34.440
(bell chiming)

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.560
- We've all been waiting for
the next productivity boom,

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:38.880
and we haven't gotten it.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.660
And we were seeing
improvement before COVID hit,

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.070
and then that changed the whole story.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:45.690
The breaking news, stay at home,

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.270
that is the order tonight
from four State Governors,

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.730
as the coronavirus pandemic spreads.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:52.830
- [Narrator] The pandemic
had significant effects

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:54.360
on worker productivity.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.490
Manufacturers and global supply chains,

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.260
operated below capacity.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.090
Ships were stuck at port,

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:02.160
and workers were stuck at home.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:03.480
To keep people spending,

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.210
the FED moved aggressively
to stimulate demand.

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:09.360
- The FED thought that
the worker productivity

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.390
would kind of go back to the
way it was before the pandemic,

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:13.380
and it didn't.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:15.960
And in the process they
overstimulated demand.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.130
So when you have too much demand

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:18.000
in too little supply,

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:18.833
you get inflation,

00:05:18.833 --> 00:05:20.400
and that's just what we got.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.890
And fundamentally, at at the root of it,

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:26.220
was because FED policy makers,

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:27.053
didn't really understand

00:05:27.053 --> 00:05:29.670
how the productivity tides were shifting,

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:32.340
once again, underneath their feet.

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.170
- [Narrator] In the first quarter of 2022,

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:37.080
productivity fell over 7%.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:40.740
Then in the second quarter,
it dropped another 4.6%.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.330
- Productivity is ultimately,

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:45.720
the source of wage growth for workers,

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:47.790
profit growth for companies,

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.410
and economic advancement.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:52.170
So it's really central to the prosperity

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:54.210
of the economy as a whole,

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:57.120
and the wellbeing of
millions of Americans.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.890
- [Narrator] Economists
say, "Drops in productivity,

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:00.840
also make it even harder for the FED

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.750
to accomplish their goal
of lowering inflation."

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:06.300
- We're not gonna know where we are,

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.880
I don't think for another
year or so at best.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:12.000
The risk is that we get
more policy mistakes

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.950
before things really do settle down.

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:16.050
- [Narrator] And as
productivity continues to shift

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:17.100
with current events,

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:19.623
it may take some time to settle down.

